WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.807
Picture the refund desk at a shop.

2
00:00:04.807 --> 00:00:09.827
A customer brings something back and gets money back, sometimes in several parts.

3
00:00:09.827 --> 00:00:14.475
The rule: the refunds for one sale must never add up to more than the sale.

4
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.114
The shop's program handles refunds on several threads.

5
00:00:18.114 --> 00:00:24.625
So the team wrote a test: two threads ask for refunds at once, and the total must stay within the sale.

6
00:00:24.625 --> 00:00:26.837
It has passed every day for a year.

7
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:33.874
Then, on the first busy day of a sale, a coat that sold for 100.00 is refunded 160.00.

8
00:00:33.874 --> 00:00:35.932
The customer is delighted.

9
00:00:35.932 --> 00:00:37.199
The shop is not.

10
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.919
So here's the question we're shy to ask.

11
00:00:39.919 --> 00:00:41.116
Was the test lying?

12
00:00:41.116 --> 00:00:44.076
And if it wasn't, what is a passing test worth?

13
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.308
The test wasn't lying.

14
00:00:46.308 --> 00:00:47.973
Its two threads never met.

15
00:00:47.973 --> 00:00:57.448
A small test like this takes a few microseconds, about as long as starting a thread, so the first thread is often done before the second one starts.

16
00:00:57.448 --> 00:01:01.417
As the race lesson put it, a race is shy around small numbers.

17
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:04.923
It's like testing the refund desk on a quiet Tuesday.

18
00:01:04.923 --> 00:01:08.522
One customer at a time, and the clerk never gets confused.

19
00:01:08.522 --> 00:01:11.253
The bug needs the first morning of the sale.

20
00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:13.838
So let's build that morning.

21
00:01:13.838 --> 00:01:19.025
A crowd waits outside, and at nine the doors open, and everyone rushes in at once.

22
00:01:19.025 --> 00:01:23.263
That's the start latch from the synchronizers lesson, a latch of 1.

23
00:01:23.263 --> 00:01:27.880
Every thread waits in await, and one countDown lets them all in together.

24
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.260
Three more habits.

25
00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:38.601
More customers than clerks: more threads than cores, the parts of the processor that each run one thread, so the system must switch between them.

26
00:01:38.601 --> 00:01:44.722
Each customer brings many returns: many operations per thread, so they overlap for a long time.

27
00:01:44.722 --> 00:01:48.265
And many sale days: many rounds, each with fresh state.

28
00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:50.760
At closing time, the till must balance.

29
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.523
That's the invariant, a fact that must hold whatever the timing.

30
00:01:54.523 --> 00:01:59.345
Check it after every thread has finished, and never check how long something took.

31
00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:06.325
The lesson ran the usual test twenty times: two threads, a thousand hits each, no start line.

32
00:02:06.325 --> 00:02:12.815
Then the stress test twenty times: eight threads, a hundred thousand hits each, and a start latch.

33
00:02:12.815 --> 00:02:14.338
Same counter, same bug.

34
00:02:14.338 --> 00:02:15.596
How many runs fail?

35
00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:21.053
The usual test failed once in twenty.

36
00:02:21.053 --> 00:02:23.338
The stress test failed all twenty.

37
00:02:23.338 --> 00:02:27.035
And in another run, the usual test never failed at all.

38
00:02:27.867 --> 00:02:30.508
Now the refund desk, under stress.

39
00:02:30.508 --> 00:02:36.489
Eight threads each try 2,000 one-cent refunds against a sale of 10,000 cents.

40
00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:39.674
A correct ledger approves exactly 10,000.

41
00:02:39.674 --> 00:02:41.927
The racy one approved 16,000.

42
00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:45.500
That's our coat: 160.00 back on a 100.00 sale.

43
00:02:46.333 --> 00:02:47.317
Two more rules.

44
00:02:47.317 --> 00:02:55.186
Give every concurrency test a timeout, so a deadlock fails in seconds instead of hanging the whole test run for an hour.

45
00:02:55.186 --> 00:02:58.595
And read a pass for what it is: not found this time.

46
00:02:58.595 --> 00:02:59.907
Never: can't happen.

47
00:03:00.733 --> 00:03:02.117
Tests also love a nap.

48
00:03:02.117 --> 00:03:06.266
Hand some work to a background thread, sleep a little, then check.

49
00:03:06.266 --> 00:03:12.930
In the lesson, eight busy threads kept every core working, like a shared test machine on a busy afternoon.

50
00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:16.514
The work took about 2 milliseconds, and the test slept 3.

51
00:03:16.514 --> 00:03:17.897
How often did it fail?

52
00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:22.294
45 times out of 100.

53
00:03:22.294 --> 00:03:26.075
A nap half as long again as the work was a coin toss.

54
00:03:26.075 --> 00:03:35.137
The latch version waits for the event itself, with 5 seconds only as a deadline: zero failures, and as fast as the work allows.

55
00:03:35.967 --> 00:03:38.457
Some races need millions of tries.

56
00:03:38.457 --> 00:03:46.805
jcstress, a Java tool from the people who build Java, runs a tiny test millions of times and counts every outcome.

57
00:03:46.805 --> 00:03:51.053
Two actors bump one refund counter and keep what they saw.

58
00:03:51.053 --> 00:03:53.177
1 and 2, or 2 and 1, is fine.

59
00:03:53.177 --> 00:03:55.520
1 and 1 means a refund was lost.

60
00:03:55.733 --> 00:04:01.235
In one run of almost two billion samples, 1 and 1 came up 36 percent of the time.

61
00:04:01.235 --> 00:04:05.581
Mark an outcome forbidden, and a single sighting fails the test.

62
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.431
The lab packs the habits into a small helper called Stress.

63
00:04:10.431 --> 00:04:13.027
Java on the left, Kotlin on the right.

64
00:04:13.027 --> 00:04:14.735
Rounds, threads and hits.

65
00:04:14.735 --> 00:04:16.375
A setup for fresh state.

66
00:04:16.375 --> 00:04:18.288
The actor every thread runs.

67
00:04:18.288 --> 00:04:19.517
And the invariant.

68
00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:22.426
So, what is a passing test worth?

69
00:04:22.426 --> 00:04:25.026
Only as much as the collisions it forced.

70
00:04:25.026 --> 00:04:30.289
Open the doors at nine, crowd the desk, run it for many days, and balance the till.

71
00:04:30.289 --> 00:04:33.587
And when it passes, read it as: not found this time.

72
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:34.636
Quick check.

73
00:04:34.636 --> 00:04:39.792
A test starts two threads, each makes one refund, and it has never failed.

74
00:04:39.792 --> 00:04:41.116
Is the ledger safe?
